\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid467257 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 To the rigid thinker, the Absurdist\rquote 
s freedom appears terrifying.
\par \'93What do you mean you don\rquote t know what comes next?!\'94 they cry. \'93How can you laugh when the rules are unclear?! Where is your structure, your order, your manual of how to live?!\'94
\par The Absurdist tilts his head, considering this, then shrugs.
\par And then, without warning, he does a cartwheel. Not because it means anything. Just because he can.
\par The rigid thinker watches in horror.\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 He does not follow the steps. He does not hold onto certainty. He is lost.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 
\par But the Absurdist is not lost. He is simply\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 not afraid to be lost.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 
\par While the rigid thinker builds higher walls, reinforcing their cage with more rules and reasons, the Absurdist climbs a tree and eats an apple upside down\emdash because why not?
\par For the rigid thinker, freedom looks like chaos. But for the Absurdist, chaos is simply life without unnecessary walls.
\par And when the rigid thinker finally grows tired of their own cage, when their fortress begins to feel like a tomb, they may finally step out and ask:\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 \'93How do you live like this?\'94}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 
\par To which the Absurdist will reply,\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid467257\charrsid467257 \'93I don\rquote t know. But it\rquote s fun.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid467257 
