{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid4223755 
An Absurdist stands at the edge of a cliff, staring into the abyss of reason, where logic and meaning swirl like storm clouds. A voice in the distance calls out, promising clarity, order, and an endless supply of moderately priced lattes. "Step into the c
age, and all will be revealed."
\par The Absurdist hesitates. The cage is shiny, but it smells like bureaucracy.
\par He takes a step forward but suddenly, a butterfly flutters past. It\rquote s majestic\emdash 
gliding effortlessly through the air, doing absolutely nothing but existing. And for a moment, the Absurdist forgets all the arguments, all the logic, all the cages.
\par He watches the butterfly, and for the first time in hours, he smiles. "Living," he mutters. "Just... living."
\par The Keepers, watching from behind a cloud of overly complex philosophy, scoff. "Intellectual weakness!" they chant in unison.
\par The Absurdist turns back to them, tilting his head. "You know, I think I'm more powerful than you. Because I\rquote m not trapped in the cage of your thinking. I\rquote m free in this moment. With this butterfly."
\par The Keepers stare at each other, unsure how to respond. They hadn\rquote t accounted for the butterfly.
\par The Absurdist, now fully convinced of the absurdity of it all, skips off into the horizon. The butterfly follows, not because it has to, but because it chooses to. And in that moment, both the Absurdist and the butterfly are, for once, utterly free.

